<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caliphate</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as caliph, caliphal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A caliphate is an Islamic state ruled by a caliph, the successor to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> as leader of the Muslim community. The principal historical caliphates were the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a>, Umayyad, Abbasid and Fatimid, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. The authority of the caliph was also invoked by many other states, such as the Seljuk, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a> sultanates, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sokoto-caliphate">Sokoto Caliphate</a> claimed the title themselves.</p></div>
